# Cloudflare static-asset header rules.
#
# The factory records themselves are a fixed snapshot of the FAST microfiche,
# so /data/ is cached long. Not `immutable` with a one-year max-age any more,
# though: that was written when /data/ held only the record files, and it has
# since gained derived files that DO get corrected as decoding improves
# (paint.json, factoryOptions.json). A returning visitor would have been stuck
# with a stale copy for up to a year, with no way to bust it short of renaming
# the file — a Cloudflare cache purge doesn't reach a browser cache. One day
# keeps repeat visits fast while letting a correction actually land.

/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# The markup and the code behind it have to move together. index.html is
# revalidated on every visit (max-age=0) and the worker's routes hold for five
# minutes, but these were held for an hour — so for up to an hour after a
# deploy a returning visitor ran new markup against an old script. That is not
# theoretical: it shipped a chassis lookup whose box rendered and whose button
# did nothing, because the HTML carrying it arrived an hour before the function
# that binds it.
#
# stale-while-revalidate made that cheap but could not close it. Two rules that
# disagree by design still disagree: the M35 removal went live and the site kept
# reporting the old record count, with the dropped chassis still listed, until
# the second page load.
#
# What closes it is version_assets.js, which stamps every reference here with a
# hash of the file's own contents — /js/app.js?v=1f4c2a9b. index.html is always
# fresh, so fresh markup asks for a URL no cache has ever seen and the pair
# cannot disagree. That is the build-step-free version of hashed filenames.
#
# These rules stay as they are rather than going long-and-immutable, which
# stamped URLs would otherwise allow. The stamp is only correct if the script
# was actually run before committing; if it was forgotten, a short max-age
# means the change still lands on its own within the week, while `immutable`
# would pin the stale file for as long as the max-age says. Cheap insurance
# against the one way this can go wrong.
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # Nothing here is meant to be embedded in someone else's page, and the site
  # has no login or form to clickjack into — DENY costs nothing.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # None of these are used by the site, so they're switched off rather than
  # left available to anything that ends up running on the page.
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
  # One year, but deliberately without includeSubDomains or preload: www does
  # not resolve yet, and both of those are painful to walk back once a browser
  # has cached them. Note this only protects visitors who have already reached
  # the site over HTTPS once — the plain-HTTP entry point still needs
  # "Always Use HTTPS" turned on in the Cloudflare dashboard.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  # No Content-Security-Policy here on purpose. The page pulls Lucide from
  # unpkg and Chart.js from jsDelivr, and leans on inline style attributes
  # throughout, so any policy strict enough to be worth having would need
  # 'unsafe-inline' anyway — and a wrong one silently breaks the charts and
  # icons on a live site. Low value against a static page with no auth, no
  # cookies and no user input that is ever stored.
